meta {
  name: Update Lead (Lead Date)
  type: http
  seq: 22
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads/{{createdLeadId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "createdDate": "2026-01-03"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("echoes the new lead date as an agency-calendar day", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().createdDate).to.equal("2026-01-03");
  });

  test("recomputes aging from the new date", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().agingDays).to.be.a("number");
    expect(res.getBody().agingDays).to.be.greaterThan(0);
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /leads/:id — re-date the lead (PAC-159 §1c)

  The business "lead received on" day, corrected after the fact. Every lead
  count, aging figure and lead-source matrix buckets `createdDate` on the
  agency's calendar at read time, so a lead typed in late *has* to be
  correctable or those figures are wrong in exactly the way the quote and sold
  dates were.

  - **Module:** `leads` · **Permission:** `leads:write` · **Scope:** the lead's.

  ## What moves
  - `createdDate`, stored at the new day's first instant in `Agency.timezone`.
  - The `lead_created` timeline row, whose meaning is "occurred on the lead date".
  - A `field_changed` row (`Lead edited`, `Lead date: 2026-01-05 → 2026-01-03`).

  ## Rules (`400`, judged on the agency's calendar; same day allowed)
  - not in the future — **Update Lead (Future Date)**;
  - not after the lead's first quote or sale — **Update Lead (After First
    Quote)** in the Quote Recaps folder, once a quote exists.

  Only a date that actually differs is checked and written: a patch that edits
  the status alone is never refused for a pre-existing (migrated) violation.

  ## Response `200`
  `UpdateLeadResult` with `createdDate` (agency day) and `agingDays`
  recomputed from it.
}
